WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.921
A payment carries a few extras.

2
00:00:04.921 --> 00:00:08.001
The accepted currencies, a list of strings.

3
00:00:08.001 --> 00:00:10.437
The retry delays, a list of longs.

4
00:00:10.437 --> 00:00:12.156
The retry count, an int.

5
00:00:12.367 --> 00:00:16.556
Put them in one map, keyed by name, and the map forgets the types.

6
00:00:16.556 --> 00:00:18.524
To it, every value is just Any.

7
00:00:18.733 --> 00:00:20.430
So every read needs a cast.

8
00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:23.760
Pick the wrong type, and the compiler can't help you.

9
00:00:24.367 --> 00:00:25.614
And names collide.

10
00:00:25.614 --> 00:00:28.177
Two teams both pick the name retries.

11
00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:30.865
The first team reads its delays back.

12
00:00:30.865 --> 00:00:31.997
What does it get?

13
00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:36.903
The retry count, an Int, where it expects a list.

14
00:00:36.903 --> 00:00:40.433
It fails at run time, far from the line that wrote it.

15
00:00:41.267 --> 00:00:47.460
The fix is a small key object that carries two things: a name, and the full type of its value.

16
00:00:47.667 --> 00:00:52.495
In Kotlin, typeOf records that type when you make the key, list of string and all.

17
00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:55.372
To write, you give a key and a value.

18
00:00:55.372 --> 00:00:58.483
The compiler checks the value against the key's type.

19
00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:02.739
Try to put 42 under the currencies key, and it doesn't compile.

20
00:01:03.367 --> 00:01:05.253
To read, you give only the key.

21
00:01:05.253 --> 00:01:09.695
Its type comes back with the value, so there is no cast at the call site.

22
00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:12.852
And the name no longer decides alone.

23
00:01:12.852 --> 00:01:18.783
Retries as a list of longs and retries as an int are two different keys, in two slots.

24
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:21.008
One cast is left, inside get.

25
00:01:21.008 --> 00:01:24.261
It's safe, because only set can put a value in.

26
00:01:24.867 --> 00:01:25.934
Last question.

27
00:01:25.934 --> 00:01:27.229
Key is invariant.

28
00:01:27.229 --> 00:01:29.516
What if you declared it out T?

29
00:01:29.733 --> 00:01:33.196
Then a key for a list of strings is also a key for Any.

30
00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:37.311
Writing 42 under it compiles, with T inferred as Any.

31
00:01:37.533 --> 00:01:39.801
Predict the next read of the currencies.

32
00:01:41.833 --> 00:01:43.195
ClassCastException.

33
00:01:43.195 --> 00:01:45.416
Integer cannot be cast to List.

34
00:01:45.416 --> 00:01:49.501
A key writes as well as reads, so it can't be a producer.

35
00:01:49.501 --> 00:01:50.863
It stays invariant.

36
00:01:51.767 --> 00:01:53.970
Here it is in Java and Kotlin.

37
00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:57.495
Java gets the full type from a super type token.

38
00:01:57.495 --> 00:01:58.891
Kotlin uses typeOf.

39
00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:02.278
The rule.

40
00:02:02.278 --> 00:02:05.999
Let the key carry the value's type, so reads need no cast.

41
00:02:05.999 --> 00:02:09.208
And keep the key invariant, because it writes too.

42
00:02:09.433 --> 00:02:10.510
Check yourself.

43
00:02:10.510 --> 00:02:16.039
With Key declared out T, which line fails: the write of 42, or the next read?

44
00:02:18.767 --> 00:02:19.414
The read.

45
00:02:19.414 --> 00:02:21.859
The write compiles, with T as Any.

46
00:02:21.859 --> 00:02:24.376
The read throws ClassCastException.
